  "question": {
    "text": "The element having the highest first ionization enthalpy is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "ynmTI",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Si</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "8KMDC",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "1ncal",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "se626",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The first ionisation energy is the energy needed to remove the outermost, or highest energy, electron from a neutral atom in the gas phase. </p><p>The ionisation energy decreases from top to bottom in groups, and increases from left to right across a period. Nitrogen and carbon belong to second period elements, hence they have more ionisation energy than the other two elements.</p><p>It is highest for nitrogen because nitrogen is a more stable half-filled electronic configuration.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Al</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>Si</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>IE</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> order.</p><p>Hence option C is the answer.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
